[Storybook interaction tests](https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/interaction-testing) let you test user interactions inside your stories. They enhance your [Storybook](https://storybook.js.org/) setup, so that your components not only look right but also behave correctly when interacted with.

## Setting up Storybook interaction tests

### Prerequisites

You need Storybook already set up in your Nx workspace. If you don't have Storybook set up yet, read the [Storybook plugin overview guide](/docs/technologies/test-tools/storybook/introduction).

### Generate Storybook configuration with interaction tests

When generating your Storybook configuration, answer `Yes` to the `Do you want to set up Storybook interaction tests?` prompt, or pass the `--interactionTests=true` flag.

```shell
nx g @nx/storybook:configuration my-project --interactionTests=true
```

For a Vite-powered framework on Storybook 10 or later, this sets up:

- The [`@storybook/addon-vitest`](https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/integrations/vitest-addon) addon in your `.storybook/main.ts`
- A `vitest.storybook.config.mts` at the project root, along with `.storybook/vitest.setup.ts`
- The `vitest`, `@vitest/browser` and `playwright` dependencies
- A `test-storybook` target that runs your stories through Vitest

The addon runs stories in a real browser, so Playwright needs its browser binaries:

```shell
npx playwright install chromium
```

The story run is kept in its own `vitest.storybook.config.mts` rather than added to your project's `vite.config` or `vitest.config`. That keeps it out of your regular `test` target, so unit tests and story tests stay separate tasks.

For Webpack-based frameworks and for Storybook 9 or earlier, Nx sets up [`@storybook/test-runner`](https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/integrations/test-runner) instead. The `test-storybook` target then runs the test runner against a served Storybook.

### Writing interaction tests

Write interaction tests in your stories using the `play` function:

```typescript
import type { Meta, StoryObj } from '@storybook/react-vite';
import { expect, userEvent, within } from 'storybook/test';

import { Button } from './button';

const meta: Meta<typeof Button> = {
  title: 'Example/Button',
  component: Button,
  parameters: {
    layout: 'centered',
  },
  argTypes: {
    backgroundColor: { control: 'color' },
  },
};

export default meta;
type Story = StoryObj<typeof meta>;

export const Primary: Story = {
  args: {
    primary: true,
    label: 'Button',
  },
  play: async ({ canvasElement }) => {
    const canvas = within(canvasElement);
    const button = canvas.getByRole('button');

    await expect(button).toBeInTheDocument();
    await userEvent.click(button);
    await expect(button).toHaveFocus();
  },
};
```

{% aside type="note" %}
Storybook 8 merged `@storybook/testing-library` and `@storybook/jest` into a single `storybook/test` entry point, and Storybook 9 folded `@storybook/addon-interactions` into the `storybook` package. Import `expect`, `userEvent` and `within` from `storybook/test`.
{% /aside %}

### Running interaction tests

Run your interaction tests with:

```shell
nx test-storybook my-project
```

### Debugging interaction tests

During development, watch your interaction tests run in the Storybook UI:

1. Start your Storybook: `nx storybook my-project`
2. Open the Interactions panel in the Storybook UI
3. Run individual story tests by clicking the play button

Step through the tests from that panel to debug any failures.
